# Line endings: LF in the repository, LF in the working tree, everywhere.
#
# Why: this repo is developed on both Windows and macOS. Before this file, a
# save on one machine could rewrite a file's endings and produce a diff where
# every line changed -- the main<->feature comparison showed 4,950 changed
# lines for 765 lines of real change, which is exactly how a real bug hides
# during review.
#
# `text=auto` normalizes to LF on commit; `eol=lf` also checks out LF, so the
# Windows and macOS working trees are byte-identical rather than
# platform-dependent. Python, git, and modern editors all read LF natively on
# Windows, so nothing needs to change in how the code is run.
#
# NOTE: this file governs future commits only. Existing CRLF files are
# renormalized on their next commit; to do them all at once, run
# `git add --renormalize . && git commit` -- planned for after
# feature/update_march_29 merges into main.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never normalize, never diff as text.
*.sqlite      binary
*.sqlite-wal  binary
*.sqlite-shm  binary
*.parquet     binary
*.png         binary
*.jpg         binary
*.jpeg        binary
*.gif         binary
*.ico         binary
*.pdf         binary
*.xlsx        binary

# Text, declared explicitly so `text=auto` never has to guess.
*.py    text
*.md    text
*.csv   text
*.json  text
*.toml  text
*.yml   text
*.yaml  text
*.sql   text
*.txt   text
